-- title:
--   Points in both component closures are crossing points
-- statement:
--   Let $p$ be a prime, let $\mathfrak{X}$ be a `DRModelPackage p` — the package of data and properties attached to the two-chart integral model `DRModel p`, the pushout of the two affine charts of the full modular function field of level $p$ with Igusa coordinate, together with its structure morphism `DRModel.toBase p` to $\operatorname{Spec}\mathbb{Z}$ — let $O$ be a commutative local ring whose maximal ideal is the ideal generated by the image of $p$, let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $p$, and let $\mathrm{to}\kappa \colon O \to \kappa$ be a ring homomorphism. Write $b =$ `DRModel.baseChangeMap toκ` for the induced morphism from the base change of `DRModel p` along $\mathbb{Z} \to \kappa$ to its base change along $\mathbb{Z} \to O$, obtained by pulling back the identity of `DRModel p` and $\operatorname{Spec}(\mathrm{to}\kappa)$, and let $i_\infty = \mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{compInf}\,\kappa$ and $i_0 = \mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{compZero}\,\kappa$ be the two morphisms from the scheme $(\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{ratModel}\,\kappa).C$ into that base change over $\kappa$. Assume that the images under $b \circ i_\infty$ and under $b \circ i_0$ of the generic point of $(\mathfrak{X}.\mathrm{ratModel}\,\kappa).C$ are distinct points. Then for every point $x$ of the base change of `DRModel p` along $\mathbb{Z} \to O$ lying both in the closure of the set-theoretic image of $b \circ i_\infty$ and in the closure of the image of $b \circ i_0$, there is a point $n$ of the fibre product of $i_\infty$ and $i_0$ with $x$ the image of $n$ under the first projection followed by $i_\infty$ followed by $b$.
--
--   In the Deligne–Rapoport description of the special fibre of $X_0(p)$, the mod $p$ fibre is the union of two rational components meeting exactly at the supersingular points; this statement says that a point of the model over $O$ lying in the closures of both components must come from the scheme-theoretic intersection of the two components, i.e. is a crossing point. It is the case of distinct component generic points in the proof of [`ModularCurve.DRModelPackage.exists_eq_baseChangeMap_crossing_of_mem_closure_of_mem_closure`](thm.html#ModularCurve.DRModelPackage.exists_eq_baseChangeMap_crossing_of_mem_closure_of_mem_closure), which removes that hypothesis.
